  "text": "« use multiple frames: always try to look at data from various angles. for\nexample, if testing shows that 90% of users like a feature, also consider the\n10% who don't, to understand the full scope of user feedback.\n\n» seek diverse opinions: consult with colleagues from different backgrounds\nor departments to get varied perspectives on the same data. this can help\nbalance out any biased interpretations.\n\n« neutral language: when gathering feedback or presenting options, use\nneutral language that doesn't lead respondents toward a particular choice.\nfor instance, rather than asking users if they \"love\" a new feature, ask how\nthey \"feel\" about it to get honest feedback.\n\n» gather more context: acknowledge when you lack enough data to make an\ninformed decision and seek additional information to better understand the\nsituation.\n\n| mn saved | | ~> share | * altutor [gy rf\nframing bias happens when people make choices based on how\ninformation is shown, not the facts themselves. to avoid this, you can do\na few things:\nfirst, look at information from different angles. for example,\n@ simplify v @ai tutor preferences\n",
